  [3mWins:[0m one record per change regardless of subscriber count; changing the subscription rules later reinterprets history
  instead of leaving it stale; you get an audit trail and a per-task activity feed for free; maps directly onto an
  [38;5;153mevents[39m table when a server lands. [3mCosts:[0m the log grows unboundedly (needs a cap or prune eventually), and "unread
  count" is a computation rather than a stored integer.

  [1mB. Fan-out-on-write, stored notification rows.[0m On each change, resolve the subscriber list and write one notification
  row per recipient, each with its own [38;5;153mread[39m flag.
  [3mWins:[0m the simplest possible read path — the inbox is a filter on one array, and unread count is a [38;5;153mlength[39m. Familiar,
  and what most tutorials show. [3mCosts:[0m changes are duplicated per subscriber; a subscription added after the fact sees
  nothing retroactively; there's no history of what happened to a task, only of what was sent to people; and this is the
  shape that hurts most at server scale, where fan-out-on-write to many subscribers is the classic write-amplification
  problem.

  [1mC. Diff two snapshots to infer changes.[0m [3mDiscarded:[0m a diff can tell you the status field changed but not who changed
  it. You need the actor to suppress self-notifications and to write "Bob moved this to Done" — so this approach can't
  express the requirements you picked.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m Two of your answers push it there specifically. You chose implicit [3mand[0m explicit subscription,
  which means the subscriber set for a task changes over time — under B, following a task shows you an empty history,
  which is exactly when someone follows a task. And you chose a user switcher, which means a single event genuinely has
  different meaning per viewer, so deriving per-viewer is the natural fit. B is a little less code today and costs a
  rewrite of the storage layer if you go multi-user.
